  <div class="blog-bc"><a href="/">Home</a> › <a href="/blog/">Blog</a> › Nominal vs Actual Lumber Dimensions</div>

  <div class="blog-header">
    <h1>Nominal vs Actual Lumber Dimensions: Board Foot Differences Explained</h1>
    <div class="blog-meta">
      <span>By <strong>James Carter</strong> | Construction &amp; Home Improvement Specialist</span>
      <span class="blog-badge">🕐 Last Updated: July 2026</span>
      <span class="blog-cat">🪵 Construction &amp; Lumber</span>
    </div>
    <p class="intro"><strong>Nominal dimensions</strong> are the labeled size on the tag — such as 2×4. <strong>Actual dimensions</strong> are the finished measurements after drying and planing — typically 1.5×3.5 inches for a standard 2×4. The gap between the two can change your board-foot calculation by 30% or more, which directly affects how much you pay at the lumberyard.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="snippet-box" id="quick-answer">
    <div class="sq">Quick Answer</div>
    <p class="sa">A nominal <strong>2×4×8</strong> = <strong>5.33 board feet</strong> (2 × 4 × 8 / 12).<br>
    The same board's actual size (1.5 × 3.5 × 8) = <strong>3.5 board feet</strong> — about 34% less.<br>
    Always confirm which dimensions your supplier uses before estimating cost.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="blog-section" id="why-different">
    <h2>Why Are Nominal and Actual Dimensions Different?</h2>
    <p>Lumber starts life at the sawmill rough-cut to its <strong>nominal size</strong>. A 2×4 is initially cut to approximately 2 inches thick and 4 inches wide. After that, the board goes through two processes that reduce its size:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Kiln drying</strong> — removing moisture causes the wood to shrink slightly.</li>
      <li><strong>Planing / surfacing (S4S)</strong> — the board is run through a planer to produce smooth, consistent faces and edges, removing another fraction of an inch from each side.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The result is a <strong>finished board</strong> that is noticeably smaller than its nominal name. The nominal name sticks because it refers to the original rough-cut size and has become the industry-standard way to identify and order lumber.</p>
    <div class="blog-info">💡 <strong>S4S</strong> stands for "Surfaced Four Side" — the most common finished lumber standard you'll find at home improvement stores like Home Depot and Lowe's.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="blog-section" id="dimension-table">
    <h2>Nominal vs Actual Lumber Size Chart</h2>
    <p>The table below shows the most common lumber sizes and their approximate actual finished dimensions.</p>
    <table class="blog-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Nominal Size</th><th>Actual Size (approx.)</th><th>Thickness Difference</th><th>Width Difference</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>1 × 2</strong></td><td>0.75 in × 1.5 in</td><td class="r">−0.25 in</td><td class="r">−0.5 in</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>1 × 3</strong></td><td>0.75 in × 2.5 in</td><td class="r">−0.25 in</td><td class="r">−0.5 in</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>1 × 4</strong></td><td>0.75 in × 3.5 in</td><td class="r">−0.25 in</td><td class="r">−0.5 in</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>1 × 6</strong></td><td>0.75 in × 5.5 in</td><td class="r">−0.25 in</td><td class="r">−0.5 in</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>1 × 8</strong></td><td>0.75 in × 7.25 in</td><td class="r">−0.25 in</td><td class="r">−0.75 in</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>1 × 10</strong></td><td>0.75 in × 9.25 in</td><td class="r">−0.25 in</td><td class="r">−0.75 in</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>1 × 12</strong></td><td>0.75 in × 11.25 in</td><td class="r">−0.25 in</td><td class="r">−0.75 in</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>2 × 2</strong></td><td>1.5 in × 1.5 in</td><td class="r">−0.5 in</td><td class="r">−0.5 in</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>2 × 4</strong></td><td>1.5 in × 3.5 in</td><td class="r">−0.5 in</td><td class="r">−0.5 in</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>2 × 6</strong></td><td>1.5 in × 5.5 in</td><td class="r">−0.5 in</td><td class="r">−0.5 in</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>2 × 8</strong></td><td>1.5 in × 7.25 in</td><td class="r">−0.5 in</td><td class="r">−0.75 in</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>2 × 10</strong></td><td>1.5 in × 9.25 in</td><td class="r">−0.5 in</td><td class="r">−0.75 in</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>2 × 12</strong></td><td>1.5 in × 11.25 in</td><td class="r">−0.5 in</td><td class="r">−0.75 in</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>4 × 4</strong></td><td>3.5 in × 3.5 in</td><td class="r">−0.5 in</td><td class="r">−0.5 in</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>4 × 6</strong></td><td>3.5 in × 5.5 in</td><td class="r">−0.5 in</td><td class="r">−0.5 in</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>6 × 6</strong></td><td>5.5 in × 5.5 in</td><td class="r">−0.5 in</td><td class="r">−0.5 in</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <div class="blog-warn">⚠️ These are standard S4S (Surfaced Four Sides) dimensions for kiln-dried construction lumber. Rough-sawn lumber, green lumber, and hardwood may have different actual dimensions.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="blog-section" id="board-foot-formula">
    <h2>Board Foot Formula</h2>
    <p>Board feet measure <strong>lumber volume</strong>, not surface area or length. One board foot equals <strong>144 cubic inches</strong>.</p>
    <h3>When length is in feet (most common)</h3>
    <div class="formula-box">Board Feet = Thickness (in) × Width (in) × Length (ft) A 12</div>
    <h3>When all dimensions are in inches</h3>
    <div class="formula-box">Board Feet = Thickness (in) × Width (in) × Length (in) A 144</div>
    <p>Use the same formula for both nominal and actual calculations — only the input numbers change. The result will differ significantly depending on which dimensions you plug in.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="blog-section" id="example-2x4">
    <h2>Worked Example: 2×4×8 Board</h2>
    <h3>Using nominal dimensions</h3>
    <div class="formula-box">2 × 4 × 8 / 12 = <strong>5.33 board feet</strong></div>
    <h3>Using actual dimensions (1.5 × 3.5 × 8)</h3>
    <div class="formula-box">1.5 × 3.5 × 8 / 12 = <strong>3.5 board feet</strong></div>
    <p>The difference is <strong>1.83 board feet</strong> — approximately <strong>34% less</strong> when using actual dimensions. For a single board that gap is manageable. For a large framing order of 200+ boards, that difference becomes hundreds of board feet and a significant pricing discrepancy.</p>

    <h3>Board-foot comparison table (8-foot boards)</h3>
    <table class="blog-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Lumber Size</th><th>Length</th><th>Nominal BF</th><th>Approx. Actual Size</th><th>Actual BF</th><th>Difference</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>2 × 4</strong></td><td>8 ft</td><td class="g">5.33</td><td>1.5 × 3.5 in</td><td>3.50</td><td class="r">−34%</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>2 × 6</strong></td><td>8 ft</td><td class="g">8.00</td><td>1.5 × 5.5 in</td><td>5.50</td><td class="r">−31%</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>2 × 8</strong></td><td>8 ft</td><td class="g">10.67</td><td>1.5 × 7.25 in</td><td>7.25</td><td class="r">−32%</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>2 × 10</strong></td><td>8 ft</td><td class="g">13.33</td><td>1.5 × 9.25 in</td><td>9.25</td><td class="r">−31%</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>2 × 12</strong></td><td>8 ft</td><td class="g">16.00</td><td>1.5 × 11.25 in</td><td>11.25</td><td class="r">−30%</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>4 × 4</strong></td><td>8 ft</td><td class="g">10.67</td><td>3.5 × 3.5 in</td><td>8.17</td><td class="r">−23%</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>1 × 6</strong></td><td>8 ft</td><td class="g">4.00</td><td>0.75 × 5.5 in</td><td>2.75</td><td class="r">−31%</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="blog-section" id="pricing-impact">
    <h2>How the Difference Affects Your Lumber Cost</h2>
    <p>If a supplier prices an <strong>8-foot 2×6</strong> by nominal board feet at <strong>$5.00 per board foot</strong>:</p>
    <h3>Supplier's calculation (nominal)</h3>
    <div class="formula-box">2 × 6 × 8 / 12 = 8 board feet → 8 × $5.00 = <strong>$40.00</strong></div>
    <h3>Your estimate using actual dimensions (incorrect method)</h3>
    <div class="formula-box">1.5 × 5.5 × 8 / 12 = 5.5 board feet → 5.5 × $5.00 = <strong>$27.50</strong></div>
    <p>The supplier's invoice will be <strong>$40.00</strong>, but your estimate was <strong>$27.50</strong> — a <strong>$12.50 gap per board</strong>. On an order of 50 boards, that is a <strong>$625 surprise</strong> on your invoice.</p>
    <div class="blog-warn">⚠️ <strong>Key rule:</strong> Always use the same dimension method as your supplier when estimating cost. Use actual dimensions only when you need to know the physical size of the finished wood — not for pricing.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="blog-section" id="when-to-use">
    <h2>When to Use Nominal vs Actual Dimensions</h2>
    <table class="blog-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Use Nominal Dimensions When…</th><th>Use Actual Dimensions When…</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Reading a lumberyard price list</td><td>Designing joints, dados, or rabbets</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Comparing standard lumber sizes</td><td>Calculating finished tabletop or shelf thickness</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Estimating board-foot cost under supplier's method</td><td>Planning cabinet or furniture dimensions</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Calculating rough-sawn board footage</td><td>Checking structural clearances</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ordering by supplier's quoted quantity</td><td>Determining how much physical material you have</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="blog-section" id="hardwood-exception">
    <h2>Important Exception: Hardwood and Rough Lumber</h2>
    <p>There is no single industry-wide rule for every lumber purchase. The nominal vs actual distinction applies mainly to <strong>standard softwood construction lumber</strong> (pine, fir, spruce). Hardwood and specialty lumber follow different conventions.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Rough-sawn hardwood</strong> is typically sold by board feet calculated from its rough dimensions before any surfacing — so the nominal and rough-sawn sizes are essentially the same at the point of sale.</li>
      <li><strong>Surfaced hardwood (S2S or S4S)</strong> will be smaller than the rough dimensions and may be priced differently by the dealer.</li>
      <li><strong>Reclaimed lumber, specialty beams, and custom-milled boards</strong> can each use their own measurement practices.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Before placing a large order, ask your supplier these questions:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Are board feet calculated from rough or finished dimensions?</li>
      <li>Are nominal or actual dimensions used for pricing?</li>
      <li>Is the price based on the full board before defects are removed?</li>
      <li>Are S4S or kiln-dried boards measured at a different size than rough stock?</li>
      <li>Is waste or trim included in the quoted quantity?</li>
    </ol>
    <div class="blog-info">💡 Hardwood dealers in cities like Portland, Cincinnati, and Charlotte often use a <strong>Doyle Log Scale</strong> or <strong>International Log Rule</strong> for pricing at the sawmill level — these are different from the standard board-foot formula used for finished lumber.</div>
  </div>

